Fillets and chamfers: a closed contact along a closed edge of its face that
passes the edge's vertex inside the stripe (mitcad#263).

The case: the inner rim of a step ring (a hole above a counterbore)
chamfered or rounded by the ring's width, the counterbore's seam turned
away from the rim's vertex. 0140 stores the contact on the ring's outer
circle, a closed edge of the counterbore's wall, and cuts the contact's
line at the circle's vertex where that lies inside the stripe. That vertex
is on the counterbore's seam, so the line's p-curve on the wall runs past
the seam: the part after it lay a period beyond the face (the wall's wire
did not close in 2d), and the topological build translated the shared
p-curve on the blend in place by the vertex's parameter for the part after
it, which moved the part before it too (their p-curves missed their edges
by the chord of the angle; an unorientable face, or for the rim in two arcs
a valid shape with p-curves off their edges).

- ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges splits the SurfData at that vertex instead
  (ChFi3d_SplitAtVertexOnLine): the part after it is a SurfData of its own
  on the same blend surface (one face, with no section between the two,
  as 0073's pieces), with lines of its own; the other line is split at the
  section through the vertex. Each part's p-curve on the face beyond is
  taken into that face's period.
- ChFi3d_PCurveInFacePeriod moves the p-curve on its face of a face
  interference by whole periods where it lies wholly outside the face's
  parametric bounds and so comes wholly inside them.

Before, the stripe of the case gave an unorientable face.

